{
  "dom_id": "step-23298",
  "name": "Example: \n                    Finding the Argument of a Complex Number in the Third Quadrant",
  "problem": "Find, in radians to two decimal places, the argument of $z=-\\sqrt{3}-\\textrm{i}.$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14226152/assets/0095750158d9a296c9950c2d753ffe820735298c96cdf4de23aef0747862658d.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the third quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $\\pi<        \\arg(z)<        \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}.$ If $\\alpha$ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta=\\arg(z) = \\pi +\\dfrac\\pi 6 = \\dfrac{7\\pi}{6}.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
